   "text": "I read the molecule as a VHL-recruiting PROTAC and assembled the SMILES region by region, left to right.\n\n- **Left end:** a 3-amino-6-(2-hydroxyphenyl)pyridazine, attached through its C4 to N3 of a 3,8-diazabicyclo[3.2.1]octane.\n- **Link to pyrimidine:** the bridging N8 of the bicycle is bonded to C2 of a pyrimidine. The pyrimidine's C5 carries a piperidin-4-yl group.\n- **Cyclohexane:** the piperidine N is bonded to a 1,4-disubstituted cyclohexane. Both substituents are drawn with hashed bonds, so I encoded them as cis (`[C@H]` \u2026 `[C@@H]`), following the drawing literally. This is the least certain part, because this kind of linker is often trans in patent structures.\n- **Linker to VHL ligand:** the cyclohexane's other substituent is CH2\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013CH(tert-butyl)\u2013C(=O). The tert-leucine carbon has plain bonds, so it gets no stereo label.\n- **VHL ligand:** the C(=O) leads into a 4-hydroxypyrrolidine-2-carboxamide with the hashed amide bond and wedged OH as drawn. I took these to be the standard (2S,4R) VHL configuration and checked the chirality tags against the VH032 SMILES. The amide N then carries a 4-(4-methylthiazol-5-yl)benzyl group.\n\nSMILES: Nc1nnc(-c2ccccc2O)cc1N1CC2CCC(C1)N2c1ncc(cn1)C1CCN(CC1)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](CC(=O)NC(C(C)(C)C)C(=O)N2C[C@H](O)C[C@H]2C(=O)NCc2ccc(cc2)-c2scnc2C)CC1"
